From a8141c06b509486b66a5011e07327a70acd7aff8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 08:35:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Micromorphic damage by kernels, on the CRTP base of the
 integrators

- The three micromorphic damage behaviour integrators (3D, 2D, anisotropic
  2D) are based on `StandardBehaviourIntegratorCRTPBase`, whose inner
  forces may now depend on the values and on the derivatives of the shape
  functions. The formulas and their order of evaluation are kept: the
  integration point by point gives the same results to the last digit
  (MicromorphicKernelsTest, before and after). They now support the
  integration by batches.
- New kinds of gradients `MICROMORPHIC_DAMAGE_3D` and
  `MICROMORPHIC_DAMAGE_2D`: the gradients are the micromorphic damage and
  its gradient, computed from the values and from the derivatives of the
  unknowns, and the tangent operator is block diagonal (`1 + D x D`
  components), read by `getTangentOperatorComponent` in the kernels of the
  stiffness matrices, of their diagonals and of the action without
  assembly. The behaviours are recognised by their structure.
- `supportsKernels` compares the kind of the gradients to the three traits
  of the behaviour integrator (values of the unknowns, derivatives of the
  unknowns, external state variable updated from the unknowns):
  `gradientsRequireUnknownsValues` and `gradientsRequireUnknownsDerivatives`
  replace `areGradientsUnknownsValues`.

Tests: MicromorphicKernelsTest compares the integration by batches and
the kernels (assembled tangent and tangent applied without assembly), with
and without elastic prediction, to the integration point by point, in 3D,
plane strain and plane stress, for MicromorphicDamageII and a new
anisotropic behaviour, OrthotropicMicromorphicDamage, rotated, whose
tangent operator is not symmetric; the damage grows, stops or saturates
depending on the points (non linear problem); differences below 5e-14, on
the host, on the debug device, on the GPU and on two processes.
MicromorphicDamageII gets a prediction operator. BatchIntegrationTest
checks the refusal of the integration by batches with a behaviour
integrator defined in the test, the micromorphic ones supporting it now.
The test suite passes with all the computations forced to the kernels
(156/156, the micromorphic tests were the last refused ones). Eight
mutations (value and gradient of the micromorphic damage, inner forces,
operator B, transposition and first block of the tangent operator,
recognition of the behaviours, rotation of the dual forces) are detected.
